 ("table", ["", "Summer (Southwest) monsoon", "Winter (Northeast) monsoon"], [
  ["Land vs ocean", "Land very hot: **low pressure**; ocean cooler: high pressure", "Land cools quickly: **high pressure**; ocean warmer: lower pressure"],
  ["Wind direction", "Ocean to land (from the southwest)", "Land to ocean (from the northeast)"],
  ["Result", "Moist winds condense into **heavy rain**", "Dry, cold winds; part crossing the **Bay of Bengal** gathers moisture and rains on **east and south India**"],
  ["Timing", "Reaches southern tip in **early June**, covers the subcontinent by **mid-July**", "Winter months"]]),
 ("ul", ["The advance is uneven: the **Western Ghats** block the winds, so their western slopes get heavy rain, while the Deccan plateau (rain-shadow side) gets less and with breaks.",
  "**Mawsynram** (Meghalaya) has the highest average annual rainfall in the world: about **11,000 mm**.",
  "Monsoons inspired ragas such as *Meghamalhar* and *Amruthavarshini*.",
  "Traditional signs: Konkan fishermen see deep-water fish near the surface; in south India the monsoon is said to come within about 50 days of the **Golden Shower (Cassia fistula)** blossoming; crows nesting high suggests little rain, low nests heavy rain. This heritage should be preserved."]),
 ("h2", "3.5 Climate and Our Lives"),
 ("ul", ["**Culture**: festivals follow seasons and farming — Baisakhi, Bihu, Pongal, Makar Sankranti, Onam, Lohri, Gudi Padwa, Chhath Puja, Hemis, Losoong etc.",
  "**Economy**: a **monsoon failure** (poor rain) hurts agriculture, forces women to walk far for water, pushes labourers to migrate to cities, and raises food prices (**inflation**). Industry also needs steady weather and water."]),
 ("h2", "3.6 Climate and Disasters"),
 ("table", ["Disaster", "What it is and causes", "Where / impact"], [
  ["Cyclone", "Intense low-pressure system over the sea; moist air rushes in and spirals inward. Calm centre is the **eye**. IMD tracks formation, path and landfall", "Eastern coast mainly; loss of life, damaged property, uprooted trees, erosion"],
  ["Flood", "Water overflows onto dry land: heavy rain run-off, rivers/lakes breaching banks. In cities: poor drainage, encroachment, concrete surfaces that stop absorption. In Himalaya: **glacial burst** when a glacial lake breaks its rock-ice barrier", "UP, Bihar, Kerala, Andhra Pradesh, Assam. **2013 Uttarakhand**: glacial burst, Kedarnath area destroyed, about 6,000 lives lost"],
  ["Landslide", "Sudden collapse of rock, soil or debris after heavy rain, earthquakes or volcanic activity; worsened by **deforestation**, unplanned construction and blocked water flow", "Himachal, Uttarakhand, Sikkim, Arunachal, Western Ghats; mostly in monsoon"],
  ["Forest fire", "Uncontrolled fire fuelled by dry weather, drought, high winds and human carelessness", "Uttarakhand, Himachal, MP, Chhattisgarh, Western Ghats; kills wildlife, harms air quality, displaces people"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Clouds** are masses of water droplets, ice crystals or both, suspended in the atmosphere.",
  "The **National Disaster Response Force (NDRF)**, trained for natural and human-made disasters, has battalions at 12 locations and does rescue and evacuation."]),
 ("h2", "3.7 Climate Change"),
 ("ul", ["**Climate change** = significant long-term shifts in temperature, precipitation and weather events. Earlier driven by natural processes; since the **19th century** largely by humans: burning **fossil fuels** (coal, oil, gas formed from ancient plants and animals), deforestation, harmful industry, wasteful consumption.",
  "The natural **greenhouse effect** (CO<sub>2</sub> and other gases trapping the Sun's heat) keeps Earth warm enough for life. Human emissions have added huge quantities in a few centuries, trapping extra heat: **global warming**.",
  "In early 2025 India's average temperature was 1-3 °C above normal, giving a short, mild winter that hurt farming and small industries.",
  "Responses: **mitigation** (cut emissions, plant trees, renewable energy, energy efficiency, sustainable lifestyles) and **resilience / adaptation** (capacity to withstand and recover). These often clash with the urge for economic growth and consumption."]),
 ("h2", "3.8 Quick Revision"),
